      "problem": "The midpoint of the line segment with endpoints $(p,-3q)$ and $(-p,7q)$\n is$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $({x}_{m},{y}_{m})$ be the midpoint between ${{(p,-3q)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(-p,7q)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{p+(-p)}{2},\\frac{-3q+7q}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{0}{2},\\frac{4q}{2}) \\\\  & =(0,2q)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
